 {"id":"fabro-3e48","title":"Implementer: add routing-consistency self-check — one route per branch","status":"open","type":"task","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:11.067Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:11.067Z","description":"In `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/implementer.md` (Inline verification report section) add: before finishing, re-read every routing instruction written — each branch must yield exactly ONE route; two routing sentences in one branch is a FAIL, fix before reporting.\n\nGrounded: implementer@1 of the basis run wrote branch (a) of `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md` with both 'route \"Already landed\"' and 'continue down the `sd ready` candidate list' sentences, making the new graph edge dead config; reviewer@1 blocked it and the avoidable pass-2 cycle (planner@2+implementer@2+tester@2+evidence@2+reviewer@2) cost ~126 s wall / ~$0.125 / 5 stage visits for a one-sentence deletion. No existing seed covers a routing self-check (searched 'routing self-check', 'one route per branch': no matches).\n\nExpected effect: label-dead-config contradictions caught at implementer time, saving ~one full review cycle (~2 min / ~$0.13) per occurrence.\n\nBasis: run 01M25HCK22PNFPARY8R3YVC9DK, workflow version 99e5a7758912f1adc63c1749584b00296293249901eafbd60fa4e0556f75e4be, commit add88af03e7532624d73a287ff9d0225a77343a8","labels":["revision"]}
 {"id":"fabro-d83a","title":"PROJECT_FACTS: name workflow.fabro as the graph source","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:11.104Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:11.104Z","description":"Add one line to `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/project-facts.md`: 'Graph source: `.fabro/workflows/develop/workflow.fabro` (DOT); `workflow.toml` wires runtime settings only — never probe it for edges.'\n\nGrounded: planner@1 of the basis run (100.6 s, $0.117 — the costliest stage of that run) burned two probes discovering this: a grep on `workflow.toml` for planner edges (seq 51–53, empty) and a `sed -n '1,120p'` head of `workflow.toml` (seq 57–59) before grepping the real file. Distinct from open fabro-0586 (available-binaries line — same file, different content) and from fabro-8d4c (probe style, not file identity).\n\nExpected effect: ~2 fewer tool calls per fresh claim; planners stop probing the wrong file every loop-asset seed.\n\nBasis: run 01M25HCK22PNFPARY8R3YVC9DK, workflow version 99e5a7758912f1adc63c1749584b00296293249901eafbd60fa4e0556f75e4be, commit add88af03e7532624d73a287ff9d0225a77343a8","labels":["revision"]}
 {"id":"fabro-9bf4","title":"Bake graphviz into the develop toolchain image","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:20.414Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T11:52:20.414Z","description":"Add `graphviz` to the apt-get list in the toolchain Dockerfile (`.fabro/Dockerfile.toolchain`).\n\nGrounded: implementer@1 of the basis run — '`dot` is not installed in this environment, so the workflow.fabro render-check fell back to eyeballing the edge block'; loop-asset seeds edit this graph regularly (that run did) and a malformed edge would only surface at the next run's engine parse. Distinct from closed fabro-05d0 (baked `gh` — different package; that saga is unrelated). Caveat already covered by closed fabro-6f6e: inert until the toolchain image rebuilds.\n\nCAPABILITY-AFFECTING (ADR-0019): this adds a tool (`dot`) to an agent-reachable surface (`.fabro/Dockerfile.toolchain`); implementation awaits explicit user approval. Proposed fix direction is engine-mediated and read-only: install the distro `graphviz` package in the existing toolchain image so implementers can run `dot -Tcanon` as a pure syntax verification of graph edits — no new credentials, no raw clients, no tokens.\n\nExpected effect: mechanical `dot -Tcanon` syntax verification of graph edits at implementer time; eyeball fallback eliminated.\n\nBasis: run 01M25HCK22PNFPARY8R3YVC9DK, workflow version 99e5a7758912f1adc63c1749584b00296293249901eafbd60fa4e0556f75e4be, commit add88af03e7532624d73a287ff9d0225a77343a8","labels":["needs-user","revision"]}
-{"id":"fabro-7627","title":"Web UI run actions: add Resume that re-runs the last failed node — engine auto-rewinds to its entry checkpoint (Retry alone discards committed stages)","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T11:17:49.617Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T12:04:39.099Z","description":"Where/what: the web run actions menu (apps/fabro-web/app/lib/run-actions.ts offers cancel/approve/deny/archive/unarchive/retry) has no Resume for terminal runs; Retry is the only recovery path and re-runs the whole workflow from scratch, discarding completed stages, stage context, and run data. Change (1) engine: a Resume of a failed run must itself determine the rewind target — the entry checkpoint of the last failed stage, dropping that stage's committed outcome/routing decision — so Resume re-runs exactly the failed node with all prior committed work intact. Plain POST /api/v1/runs/{id}/start {\"resume\":true} is NOT enough when the failure already committed a routing decision (soft-exit park): it replays only the exit stage and re-parks immediately. (2) web UI: expose this as a Resume action in the run actions menu for terminal runs that have checkpoints; Retry stays for explicit fresh starts and the UI copy must distinguish the two. Optional advanced form: a 'Rewind to checkpoint…' picker over the checkpoint timeline (node@visit, as fabro rewind --list shows). (3) API changes OpenAPI-first (docs/public/api-reference/fabro-api.yaml -> fabro-api build.rs -> fabro-api-client regen). Open design point: fabro rewind forks a new run id ('new run 01M25G94'); one-click Resume either keeps fork+parent link or the engine grows in-place resume-from-failure for terminal runs. Expected effect: a 429-style mid-run death costs only the failed node's re-execution instead of the whole run. Grounded: 2026-09-10 zai 429 killed Revisor 01M257B996MJ at the File stage after 'Findings ready' (~9 min agent work, checkpoint @3 analyze); UI Retry would have discarded it. Plain `fabro resume` re-ran only the exit stage and re-parked in 2s (routing 'Bookkeeping failed (nothing merged)' was already checkpointed); the actual rescue needed `rewind @3` (fork 01M25G94TMN8) + resume -> File stage succeeded on first attempt, PR #118. Basis: run 01M257B996MJNPWPY33MNDAEHP (+ fork 01M25G94TMN81J03TC153NRJXF, PR #118), 2026-09-10","labels":["web","engine"],"assignee":"fabro"}
+{"id":"fabro-7627","title":"Web UI run actions: add Resume that re-runs the last failed node — engine auto-rewinds to its entry checkpoint (Retry alone discards committed stages)","status":"in_progress","type":"feature","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T11:17:49.617Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T14:42:39.784Z","description":"Where/what: the web run actions menu (apps/fabro-web/app/lib/run-actions.ts offers cancel/approve/deny/archive/unarchive/retry) has no Resume for terminal runs; Retry is the only recovery path and re-runs the whole workflow from scratch, discarding completed stages, stage context, and run data. Change (1) engine: a Resume of a failed run must itself determine the rewind target — the entry checkpoint of the last failed stage, dropping that stage's committed outcome/routing decision — so Resume re-runs exactly the failed node with all prior committed work intact. Plain POST /api/v1/runs/{id}/start {\"resume\":true} is NOT enough when the failure already committed a routing decision (soft-exit park): it replays only the exit stage and re-parks immediately. (2) web UI: expose this as a Resume action in the run actions menu for terminal runs that have checkpoints; Retry stays for explicit fresh starts and the UI copy must distinguish the two. Optional advanced form: a 'Rewind to checkpoint…' picker over the checkpoint timeline (node@visit, as fabro rewind --list shows). (3) API changes OpenAPI-first (docs/public/api-reference/fabro-api.yaml -> fabro-api build.rs -> fabro-api-client regen). Open design point: fabro rewind forks a new run id ('new run 01M25G94'); one-click Resume either keeps fork+parent link or the engine grows in-place resume-from-failure for terminal runs. Expected effect: a 429-style mid-run death costs only the failed node's re-execution instead of the whole run. Grounded: 2026-09-10 zai 429 killed Revisor 01M257B996MJ at the File stage after 'Findings ready' (~9 min agent work, checkpoint @3 analyze); UI Retry would have discarded it. Plain `fabro resume` re-ran only the exit stage and re-parked in 2s (routing 'Bookkeeping failed (nothing merged)' was already checkpointed); the actual rescue needed `rewind @3` (fork 01M25G94TMN8) + resume -> File stage succeeded on first attempt, PR #118. Basis: run 01M257B996MJNPWPY33MNDAEHP (+ fork 01M25G94TMN81J03TC153NRJXF, PR #118), 2026-09-10","labels":["web","engine"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-9837","title":"Reviewer blob refs: state payload byte size in the marker; make a bounded nu substring read the mandatory first action","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T13:09:17.374Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T13:15:19.104Z","description":"In run 01M25K41V4NEWXSA3SVGRHKRXK the 62,474-byte evidence capture reached the reviewer as a blob ref; the first read_file landed mid-file truncated (~14 KB omitted) and recovery required nu -c 'open --raw <path> | str substring' (reviewer journal 12:56:51). Change: (1) make the engine's blob-ref/demotion marker state the payload byte size; (2) rewrite the LARGE VALUES paragraph in .fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/reviewer.md to mandate 'nu -c \"open --raw <path> | str substring 0..20000\"' as the first action before any read_file. Supersedes fabro-a0fe (read_file offset/limit is exactly what truncated here); budget raises stay as filed (fabro-8d2c, fabro-cf3e). Expected effect: one-shot evidence reads; removes the unread-blob verification-blocked bounce (~2-4 min plus a second reviewer visit). Basis: run 01M25K41V4NEWXSA3SVGRHKRXK, workflow version e825d8c827ce15defe0aa57d3586b0f369cab48cc7fe711d526776762f442aa1, commit 61d04c38f89a54168e02a12c4b1f82b0dc9c026b","labels":["revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-8a60","title":"Qualitygate: verify seed-spec-named loop-asset paths appear in the run diff","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T12:28:38.542Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T12:28:38.542Z","description":"In `scripts/qualitygate.nu`, extend the loop-asset tier (landed via fabro-bfe1) with one deterministic check: every path the in-progress seed's spec names that lies under the loop-asset roots must appear in the run diff (empty diff = red). Expected effect: an empty-edit or wrong-file pass bounces at the tester in seconds instead of consuming a full review cycle. Basis: run 01M25732658J2RFTM6MDM8CPFG, workflow version 38bab0b8b1faad62aaddb5e68facf6f1c58286169676479d3edbf99d4205b91c, commit 1c3dec49a41bd92afdd64a510210734cfc301b63","labels":["revision"]}
 {"id":"fabro-895d","title":"Engine: staleness supervisor retires run PRs on .seeds JSONL update-conflicts — revisor bookkeeping lost (resumed/forked runs carry stale tracker state)","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T12:29:07.847Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T12:29:07.847Z","description":"PROBLEM: the PR staleness supervisor closes run PRs as stale_base after 3 update-branch attempts fail with 422. When the conflict sits in `.seeds/issues.jsonl`, the branch is unmergeable by GitHub's updater and the run's whole bookkeeping (filed seeds, review/revision artifacts, journals) is dropped from base when the PR retires. EVIDENCE (2026-09-10): revisor run 01M257B996MJ died on the zai 429 at its File stage; rescued via `fabro rewind @3` into fork 01M25G94TMN8, whose PR #118 (two seeds + review of develop run 01M25732658J) sat in the gate while base moved (#117 merge + seeds syncs). Supervisor update-branch attempts hit 422 on the tracker file at 11:16/11:21/11:26 (attempts 1-3), PR closed stale_base 11:31:34. The two revisor seeds and the review artifacts landed nowhere; manual recovery from run branch `fabro/run/01M25G94TMN81J03TC153NRJXF` during integrate (seed re-filed as fabro-8a60, second folded into fabro-52b4). ROOT CAUSE: forked/resumed runs inherit the pre-fork clone's tracker state; a normal base move then conflicts at JSONL level, which neither GitHub update-branch nor the supervisor can resolve. CHANGE (engine, fabro-server pull_request_supervisor / pipeline): (a) resolve 422 conflicts on `.seeds/issues.jsonl`/`.mulch/**` with the documented closed-wins union instead of counting a strike; or (b) rebase tracker state onto current base at fork/resume creation; or (c) at minimum, park (not close) revisor/bookkeeping PRs on retirement and surface the loss — silent drop of filed findings is the worst outcome. CLASS LINEAGE: fabro-94e8 (origin: run-branch staleness) -> fabro-17e8 (409 must not strike) -> this (422-on-JSONL real conflict must resolve, not strike). One class, one durable fix: make the supervisor conflict-aware for the loop's own JSONL formats. Basis: runs 01M257B996MJNPWPY33MNDAEHP + fork 01M25G94TMN81J03TC153NRJXF, PR #118, server log 11:16-11:31 UTC, 2026-09-10","labels":["engine","workflows"]}
